      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\), we need to determine how many times 3 appears as a factor in the numbers from 1 to 24. This is done by counting the multiples of 3, 3^2 (9), 3^3 (27, since 27 is greater than 24), and so on.\n\n1. Count the multiples of 3:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 8\n\\]\nSo, there are 8 multiples of 3 in the numbers from 1 to 24.\n\n2. Count the multiples of 9 (since \\(3^2 = 9\\)):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{9} \\right\\rfloor = 2\n\\]\nSo, there are 2 multiples of 9.\n\n3. Count the multiples of 27 (since \\(3^3 = 27\\)), but since 27 is greater than 24, there are no multiples of 27 in the numbers from 1 to 24.\n\nAdding these together gives the total exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\):\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\nThus, the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
